#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115121 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c1cc6b6-5253-513c-98b0-9a963895eab6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/id #uuid "7d530adb-8bcc-53f1-b1bf-674be8f7046e" :question/math-academy-id "e-589" :question/problem "![](images/70/70dc1594c0072b83429fc1f5d6be4fa454f6650c3eeef37c41c2d3ec691bb35f.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $y=x^2$ and the horizontal line $y=-1$ from $x=0$ to $x=1$, as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $360^{\\circ}$about the line $y=-1$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/3b/3b0427e7d37245d86d347d6d4f867ae3d17e5f0a030feb53d28ad4bbc856f85c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-1$, and\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x^2$.\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} V &= \\pi \\int_{0}^{1} \\big[\\: \\left({\\color{blue}\\textrm{upper function}}\\right) - \\left({\\color{brown}\\textrm{lower function}}\\right) \\:\\big]^2\\,\\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &= \\pi \\int_{0}^{1} \\left[\\: {\\color{blue}x^2}- \\left({\\color{brown}-1} \\right)\\:\\right]^2\\,\\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &= \\pi\\int_{0}^{1} (x^2+1)^2 \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &= \\pi\\int_{0}^{1} (x^4+2x^2+1) \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &= \\left.\\pi \\left(\\dfrac{x^5}{5}+\\dfrac{2x^3}{3}+x \\right) \\Bigg\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\[5pt] &= \\pi \\left(\\left[ \\dfrac{1}{5}+\\dfrac{2}{3}+1 \\right] - 0 \\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{28 \\pi}{15}. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115122 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42ad968e-05d7-5eb6-92aa-c6d4221319a4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/id #uuid "a02ed650-9bbd-552b-9d80-c2d8f1d581b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-590" :question/problem "![](images/e9/e99f6cd10a72c91e5c7430a9ee18a45ba4dc09f941c779077567436a5fe90405.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x-1}$, the vertical line $x=1$, and the horizontal line $y=1$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $x=1$, as shown in the picture. Find an integral expression for the volume of the resulting solid." :question/worked-solution "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y$:\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{x-1} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=y^3+1\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.\n\n![](images/73/73fc04f5a8a56b0faba6ec2a77f8aafd864c84786adfe7da9ccda99d0c8a4d8f.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=1$, and\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=y^3+1$.\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{0}^{1}[(right function) - (left function)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}[(y^{3} + 1) - (1)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}(y^{3})^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}y^{6}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115126 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115301 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d402001-2d71-5813-9732-fcc950a7ae1b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/id #uuid "413e2100-cec8-5c20-981a-73b9a61ddbc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7148" :question/problem "![](images/40/401368d830825a55e27fc6ba191a1c3dbb80d93b2e0af74db3337523817d2c65.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\dfrac 3 2x+\\dfrac 1 2$, for $x\\in [1,3]$, and the horizontal line $y=1$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $y=1$, as shown above. Find an integral that represents the volume of the resulting solid." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=1$ to $x=3$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/04/0434e16502f9b20bb53f3c46ba265239c59e8f5a487cdf3e71962fc0019a927d.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=1$, and\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\dfrac 3 2x+\\dfrac 1 2$.\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{1}^{3}[(upper function) - (lower function)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{1}^{3}[(\\frac{3}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}) - (1)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{1}^{3}(\\frac{3}{2}x - \\frac{1}{2})^{2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115127 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115302 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f948be97-572c-5e4f-a5fa-1291cefbb4ac" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/id #uuid "4da6aa8a-844d-53c4-b573-2f81acb5a5c4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7149" :question/problem "Consider the region bounded by the curve $x=\\sqrt{y}$, the vertical line $x=2$, and the horizontal line $y=4$, as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $x=2$.\n\n![](images/a5/a56f30e5912629c9854e68d4837a37dae55a005e98ef6cf5379b5eb81022afb7.png)" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine the limits of integration. To do so, we find the intersections of the curve and the line $x=2$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{y} &= 2 \\\\\ny &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=4$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.\n\n![](images/07/0748c87aa004f06f99a19ab4b7a2dadcf2f86920b53d1edd7ff19aced1d76f04.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=\\sqrt{y}$, and\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=2$.\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{0}^{4}[(right function) - (left function)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{4}[(2) - (\\sqrt{y})]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{4}[2 - \\sqrt{y}]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{4}(4 - 4\\sqrt{y} + y)dy \\\\\n&= π[4y - \\frac{8}{3}y^{3/2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{4} \\\\\n&= π[(4(4) - \\frac{8}{3}(4)^{3/2} + ((4)^{2})/(2)) - (0)] \\\\\n&= π[16 - \\frac{64}{3} + 8] \\\\\n&= \\frac{8π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
